  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAlthough a relative lull in activity is in place as of this\nwriting, additional showers and thunderstorms have formed in\nOhio, in advance of a shortwave in the general Great Lakes/NE\nCONUS trough. This activity should spread eastward through the\nmorning, impacting the Upper Ohio Valley especially between 07Z\nand 12Z. \n\nSevere threat with this activity should remain quite low with\nlittle low-level instability in place. However, the column will\nremain favorable for efficient rainfall production and high\nprecipitation rates. Precipitable water values will remain in\nthe 1.7 to 1.8 inch range in the ongoing southwest flow. Near-\nterm model soundings show warm cloud depths of 10k-12k feet\nalong with decent low-level saturation. Although individual\ncells will have some motion, some training is possible as mean\nflow may align with a WSW-oreinted low level jet, suggesting\nsome training is possible. The 00Z HREF suggests some 1-2\" 6-hour\nmaximum rainfall totals are possible, which could be an issues\nif such totals hit the wrong areas, such as the locations near\nand east of Pittsburgh that received heavier rain on Friday, and\nthose areas will be monitored closely. Still, the threat of \nflooding should remain too isolated and for too short a period \nfor the issuance of a watch.\n\nMost of this activity should depart after 13Z or so with the\ninitial shortwave. After a lull, another round of likely more\nscattered showers and storms is expected during the late\nafternoon through around midnight or so with another shortwave\nin the flow. With a drier overall column by that point and\ncorrespondingly more subdued PWAT of 1.2 to 1.5 inches, the\nheavy rain threat should be a little lower. Nevertheless, that\ndrier air aloft will help tick up the threat of gusty to \nborderline severe winds, as 1500-2000 J/kg of surface-based \nCAPE and around 1000 J/kg of downdraft CAPE appear likely at\nleast into the early to middle evening hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe second round should end by 06Z tonight, and then a needed \nbreak still appears likely through Sunday and Sunday night. \nModel soundings continue to suggest a drier column and mid-level\ncapping that would prevent convective development. Dewpoints in\nthe mid and upper 60s, after today's readings in the 70s, \nshould lend a less sticky feel to the air. \n\nThe overall trough remains in place through much of next week. \nDaily showers and thunderstorm chances remain in the forecast \nfor Monday through at least Thursday, as several shortwave \nripples continue to ride through on the flow. Heavy rain \npotential will need continued scrutiny, as precipitable water \nvalues remain above-normal in a climatologic sense. WPC will \nlikely place our region in a Slight (level 2 of 4) Risk for \nexcessive rainfall on that day.\n\nSevere weather chances show an uptick as well, particularly\nearlier in the week according to the long-range machine-learning\nconvective guidance. This makes sense, as this period shows the\nbest juxtaposition of instability, moderate deep-layer shear,\nand decent low-level flow. Some uncertainty remains among the\nvarious ensemble members that will be better resolved in time.",
